      "id": 1,
      "text": "Le Raysville was named after Frenchman James Le Ray de Chaumont, who bought 80,000 acres in eastern Bradford County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Raysville%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough’s historic LeRaysville Cheese Factory still churns Amish‑made cheese from local farmers’ milk.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradfordcountypa.gov/212/LeRaysville-Borough",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Main Street, also known as State Route 467, cuts north‑south through Le Raysville and has been the town’s main thoroughfare since its founding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradfordcountypa.gov/212/LeRaysville-Borough",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Le Raysville split from Pike Township in 1863, becoming its own borough amid a wave of local governance changes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradfordcountypa.gov/212/LeRaysville-Borough",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Two streams, Ford Street Creek and Rockwell Creek, flow through the borough, shaping its modest waterways.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradfordcountypa.gov/212/LeRaysville-Borough",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
